1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,020
You hand over a hundred
dollars at the checkout.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:07,000
Bread, coffee, a gallon of
milk, a week of dinners.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,760
The scanner beeps, the receipt prints, and
the money disappears into the register.

4
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:16,540
So how much of that hundred dollars
does the supermarket actually keep?

5
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:22,780
At Kroger, which runs nearly 2,700
supermarkets across the United States,

6
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:28,850
the answer for its latest fiscal year
was about $1.28 in operating profit.

7
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:34,924
And yet the same company brought in
more than $147 billion in sales.

8
00:00:35,304 --> 00:00:36,284
Tiny margins.

9
00:00:36,284 --> 00:00:37,894
Enormous numbers.

10
00:00:37,894 --> 00:00:40,144
How does that business actually work?

11
00:00:41,044 --> 00:00:43,944
Start with the scale, because
it changes everything.

12
00:00:44,324 --> 00:00:48,444
In the fiscal year that
ended in January 2026,

13
00:00:48,444 --> 00:00:53,724
Kroger reported $147.6 billion in sales.

14
00:00:53,724 --> 00:01:00,473
Its operating profit was $1.9
billion, or 1.28 percent of sales.

15
00:01:00,853 --> 00:01:08,233
That year included a $2.5 billion charge
tied to its automated fulfillment network.

16
00:01:08,233 --> 00:01:13,292
The year before, operating profit
was 2.62 percent of sales.

17
00:01:13,672 --> 00:01:15,892
Either way, the pattern is the same:

18
00:01:15,892 --> 00:01:19,072
one or two cents of operating
profit on each dollar,

19
00:01:19,072 --> 00:01:22,163
multiplied across a mountain of sales.

20
00:01:22,543 --> 00:01:29,043
Even one percent of nearly $150
billion is well over a billion dollars.

21
00:01:29,423 --> 00:01:33,443
Scale works the same way at
the top of the industry.

22
00:01:33,443 --> 00:01:39,233
Walmart's operating income was 4.2 percent
of net sales in its latest fiscal year.

23
00:01:39,233 --> 00:01:46,074
On more than $700 billion of net sales,
that came to nearly $30 billion.

24
00:01:46,454 --> 00:01:48,914
That is the first secret
of the supermarket.

25
00:01:48,914 --> 00:01:51,994
It is not built to earn
a lot on each sale.

26
00:01:51,994 --> 00:01:55,024
It is built to make an
enormous number of them.

27
00:01:55,924 --> 00:01:58,024
To see where the rest goes,

28
00:01:58,024 --> 00:02:01,984
follow a hundred dollars of Kroger's
sales through its income statement.

29
00:02:02,364 --> 00:02:07,564
First, the cost of the goods
themselves, including advertising,

30
00:02:07,564 --> 00:02:09,504
warehousing and transportation.

31
00:02:09,504 --> 00:02:12,904
That takes about $77.14.

32
00:02:13,284 --> 00:02:17,904
What remains is gross profit: $22.86.

33
00:02:17,904 --> 00:02:22,504
Kroger reports a gross
margin of 22.9 percent.

34
00:02:22,884 --> 00:02:26,334
Gross margin is what survives
the cost of the product.

35
00:02:26,334 --> 00:02:28,064
It is not profit yet.

36
00:02:28,444 --> 00:02:35,064
Next comes the cost of running the business:
wages and benefits, utilities, rent,

37
00:02:35,064 --> 00:02:39,874
depreciation, and that
year's $2.5 billion charge.

38
00:02:39,874 --> 00:02:42,791
Together, about $21.58.

39
00:02:43,171 --> 00:02:49,351
What's left is operating profit, what
the business itself earned: $1.28.

40
00:02:49,731 --> 00:02:52,711
Then interest, taxes and other items.

41
00:02:52,711 --> 00:02:58,091
Net earnings, the bottom line for
shareholders, came to about 69 cents.

42
00:02:58,471 --> 00:03:02,161
Gross margin, operating
margin, net margin.

43
00:03:02,161 --> 00:03:05,411
Three different numbers that
answer three different questions,

44
00:03:05,411 --> 00:03:10,031
and mixing them up is the easiest
way to misread a supermarket.

45
00:03:10,931 --> 00:03:14,851
Inside those averages, every
product plays a different role.

46
00:03:14,851 --> 00:03:17,271
Here is a hypothetical basket.

47
00:03:17,271 --> 00:03:22,511
These prices and costs are illustrative,
not data from any real store.

48
00:03:22,891 --> 00:03:25,981
Ten items, $54 at the register.

49
00:03:26,361 --> 00:03:29,381
In this example, the milk
earns almost nothing.

50
00:03:29,381 --> 00:03:35,001
Stores sometimes price staples like this
aggressively, even as loss leaders,

51
00:03:35,001 --> 00:03:39,881
sold at a thin margin or below cost
to bring shoppers through the door.

52
00:03:40,261 --> 00:03:44,801
The store-brand cereal earns more than
the national brand sitting next to it.

53
00:03:44,801 --> 00:03:47,931
The prepared meal from
the deli earns more still.

54
00:03:48,311 --> 00:03:53,591
Add it up, and this basket's gross
margin lands near 23 percent.

55
00:03:53,591 --> 00:03:57,471
We chose the numbers so it would
roughly match Kroger's reported figure.

56
00:03:57,851 --> 00:04:02,541
The point is that a supermarket does
not need every item to be profitable.

57
00:04:02,541 --> 00:04:05,171
It needs the mix of what you buy to be.

58
00:04:06,071 --> 00:04:07,731
The second secret is speed.

59
00:04:08,111 --> 00:04:12,541
Much of what a grocery store sells
can spoil, so it cannot sit for long.

60
00:04:12,541 --> 00:04:17,691
Trucks arrive, pallets move through
the stockroom, shelves are refilled,

61
00:04:17,691 --> 00:04:20,431
and the same shelf space is emptied again.

62
00:04:20,811 --> 00:04:26,491
One way to measure this is inventory turnover:
the cost of the goods sold in a year,

63
00:04:26,491 --> 00:04:29,869
divided by the average inventory on hand.

64
00:04:30,249 --> 00:04:34,229
Using figures from their
latest annual reports,

65
00:04:34,229 --> 00:04:39,469
Kroger's inventory turned over roughly
twelve times, or about once a month.

66
00:04:39,469 --> 00:04:41,709
Costco's turned about fourteen times.

67
00:04:41,709 --> 00:04:46,633
Walmart's, with far more general
merchandise, about nine times.

68
00:04:47,013 --> 00:04:50,333
Twelve turns a year means the
average item spends about a

69
00:04:50,333 --> 00:04:53,063
month between the delivery
truck and the checkout.

70
00:04:53,063 --> 00:04:58,413
The faster that cycle runs, the less
cash sits on the shelves at any moment.

71
00:04:58,793 --> 00:05:02,433
Every turn is another chance to
earn that thin margin again,

72
00:05:02,433 --> 00:05:06,683
from the same shelf space and
the same money tied up in stock.

73
00:05:07,583 --> 00:05:09,403
And this is where it gets interesting.

74
00:05:09,403 --> 00:05:13,023
At the end of their latest
fiscal years, Walmart,

75
00:05:13,023 --> 00:05:16,503
Costco and Kroger each owed
more in accounts payable

76
00:05:16,503 --> 00:05:19,426
than the value of the
inventory on their books.

77
00:05:19,806 --> 00:05:23,086
Accounts payable are bills
that haven't been paid yet.

78
00:05:23,086 --> 00:05:28,026
When goods sell before their invoices
come due, suppliers are, in effect,

79
00:05:28,026 --> 00:05:30,272
helping to finance the shelves.

80
00:05:30,652 --> 00:05:33,092
Here is a simple hypothetical.

81
00:05:33,092 --> 00:05:38,032
A case of pasta arrives on day
one, sells out within three weeks,

82
00:05:38,032 --> 00:05:41,112
and the supplier's invoice
is due on day thirty.

83
00:05:41,112 --> 00:05:42,622
For more than a week,

84
00:05:42,622 --> 00:05:46,592
the store holds the shoppers'
money before it pays for the pasta.

85
00:05:46,972 --> 00:05:49,282
Money also flows the other way.

86
00:05:49,282 --> 00:05:54,592
Walmart's annual report describes payments
from suppliers for volume incentives,

87
00:05:54,592 --> 00:05:58,932
warehouse allowances, markdowns
and certain advertising,

88
00:05:58,932 --> 00:06:02,631
and records them as a
reduction of its cost of sales.

89
00:06:03,011 --> 00:06:06,851
So the gross margin you see is
shaped not only by shelf prices,

90
00:06:06,851 --> 00:06:10,401
but by negotiations most
shoppers never see.

91
00:06:11,301 --> 00:06:13,381
Then there is the store's own brand.

92
00:06:13,761 --> 00:06:21,941
Kroger says its Our Brands products made up more
than $39 billion of its sales in fiscal 2025,

93
00:06:21,941 --> 00:06:28,267
and that its supermarkets stock, on average,
more than 13,000 private label items.

94
00:06:28,647 --> 00:06:31,067
That is roughly a
quarter of all its sales.

95
00:06:31,447 --> 00:06:37,287
Costco says its Kirkland Signature products are
generally priced lower than national brands,

96
00:06:37,287 --> 00:06:39,997
and generally earn higher margins.

97
00:06:40,377 --> 00:06:42,597
Both can be true at once.

98
00:06:42,597 --> 00:06:46,517
When the retailer controls the
product, the label and the price,

99
00:06:46,517 --> 00:06:50,887
it can charge the shopper less and
still keep a larger share of each sale.

100
00:06:51,787 --> 00:06:52,967
Now the danger.

101
00:06:52,967 --> 00:06:56,599
In a business this thin,
small losses are big problems.

102
00:06:56,979 --> 00:07:01,059
Retailers call it shrink: products
that were paid for but never sold,

103
00:07:01,059 --> 00:07:04,339
because they spoiled, were
damaged, were stolen,

104
00:07:04,339 --> 00:07:07,069
or simply went missing in the records.

105
00:07:07,449 --> 00:07:12,709
Groceries carry extra risk because
so much of the store is perishable.

106
00:07:12,709 --> 00:07:13,509
The U.S.

107
00:07:13,509 --> 00:07:17,529
Department of Agriculture estimated
that food losses at the retail

108
00:07:17,529 --> 00:07:22,039
level equaled 10 percent of the
available food supply in 2010.

109
00:07:22,039 --> 00:07:25,629
It is an older estimate,
but it shows the scale.

110
00:07:26,009 --> 00:07:30,439
That is also why perishables get marked
down near the end of their shelf life.

111
00:07:30,439 --> 00:07:35,379
A bag of salad sold at half price
still recovers part of its cost.

112
00:07:35,379 --> 00:07:38,089
One thrown away recovers nothing.

113
00:07:38,469 --> 00:07:41,369
Here is why it matters so much.

114
00:07:41,369 --> 00:07:44,929
Imagine a store with a 2
percent operating margin.

115
00:07:44,929 --> 00:07:48,289
To make up for one dollar
of product lost to shrink,

116
00:07:48,289 --> 00:07:51,059
it needs about fifty dollars of new sales.

117
00:07:51,439 --> 00:07:53,899
That is why Kroger listed
lower shrink among the

118
00:07:53,899 --> 00:07:57,449
reasons its gross margin
improved last year.

119
00:07:58,349 --> 00:08:01,899
Not every company that sells
groceries plays the same game.

120
00:08:02,279 --> 00:08:05,599
Walmart is a general merchandise giant.

121
00:08:05,599 --> 00:08:11,839
In its latest fiscal year, grocery
was about 59 percent of Walmart U.S.

122
00:08:11,839 --> 00:08:13,259
sales.

123
00:08:13,259 --> 00:08:18,159
Its consolidated gross profit
was 24.2 percent of net sales,

124
00:08:18,159 --> 00:08:21,519
and operating income was 4.2 percent.

125
00:08:21,899 --> 00:08:25,119
Costco runs membership warehouse clubs.

126
00:08:25,119 --> 00:08:30,819
Its gross margin on net
sales was just 11.09 percent.

127
00:08:30,819 --> 00:08:35,467
But it also collected $5.9
billion in membership fees.

128
00:08:35,847 --> 00:08:40,887
Those fees were equal to about half of
Costco's operating income for the year.

129
00:08:40,887 --> 00:08:46,557
Its model pairs very thin margins on
goods with a fee that members pay to shop.

130
00:08:46,937 --> 00:08:52,487
A traditional supermarket chain like Kroger
leans on fresh food, its own brands,

131
00:08:52,487 --> 00:08:57,377
pharmacy, fuel and growing side
businesses such as advertising.

132
00:08:57,757 --> 00:09:00,177
So these numbers are not interchangeable.

133
00:09:00,177 --> 00:09:08,607
A 24 percent gross margin at Walmart and an 11 percent
gross margin at Costco describe different businesses,

134
00:09:08,607 --> 00:09:10,941
not better or worse ones.

135
00:09:11,841 --> 00:09:15,521
So, how do supermarkets
make money on tiny margins?

136
00:09:15,901 --> 00:09:17,901
By selling an enormous volume.

137
00:09:17,901 --> 00:09:21,091
By turning their inventory
over again and again.

138
00:09:21,091 --> 00:09:24,771
By using supplier credit to
help finance their shelves.

139
00:09:24,771 --> 00:09:26,891
By leaning on their own brands.

140
00:09:26,891 --> 00:09:30,861
By fighting shrink, one
damaged box at a time.

141
00:09:30,861 --> 00:09:35,631
And, in some models, by charging for
membership or selling advertising.

142
00:09:36,011 --> 00:09:40,531
The next time you hand over that hundred
dollars, remember: at a chain like Kroger,

143
00:09:40,531 --> 00:09:44,871
the company keeps only about one to
three dollars as operating profit.

144
00:09:44,871 --> 00:09:48,761
The rest pays for the food, the people,

145
00:09:48,761 --> 00:09:52,061
the buildings and the trucks
that make a supermarket work.

146
00:09:52,441 --> 00:09:54,001
This is Hidden Ledger.

147
00:09:54,001 --> 00:09:57,001
Every number in this video is
sourced in the description.

